<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Louis Stevenson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1850">1850</a>–<a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Louis Stevenson was a Scottish novelist, author of Treasure Island and Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde. From 1888 he travelled in the Pacific and wrote about the Gilbertese king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tem-binoka">Tem Binoka</a> of Abemama. He settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vailima">Vailima</a> in Samoa, where he criticised German, British and American interference in A Footnote to History, and he died there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-louis-stevenson.jpg" alt="Robert Louis Stevenson" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henry Walter Barnett · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Louis_Stevenson_by_Henry_Walter_Barnett_bw.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Robert Louis Stevenson</b>, after hearing the Jacobite song sung by a visitor, probably in 1885, judged its words &quot;unworthy&quot; and wrote new verses &quot;more in harmony with the plaintive tune&quot;, published in 1892.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-skye-boat-song">The Skye Boat Song</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Robert Louis Stevenson</b>, who stayed on the island in 1889, described Binoka in 'In the South Seas' as a shrewd and forceful ruler.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-abemama">Kingdom of Abemama</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Robert Louis Stevenson</b>, living at Vailima, wrote against foreign meddling.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-samoa">Kingdom of Samoa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Vailima, in what is now <a href="/countries/samoa">Samoa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1878" aria-label="Edinburgh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4481 258 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Louis Stevenson"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 528h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4931 528h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.95,-3.19&amp;year=1878">What was here in 1878? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Little do ye know your own blessedness; for to travel hopefully is a better thing than to arrive, and the true success is to labour.&#8221;</p><footer>El Dorado (Virginibus Puerisque, 1881) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/386/pg386-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Extreme busyness, whether at school or college, kirk or market, is a symptom of deficient vitality; and a faculty for idleness implies a catholic appetite and a strong sense of personal identity.&#8221;</p><footer>An Apology for Idlers (Virginibus Puerisque, 1881) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/386/pg386-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Give us courage and gaiety and the quiet mind. Spare to us our friends, soften to us our enemies.&#8221;</p><footer>Prayers Written at Vailima, Samoa (1904) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/616/pg616-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;This be the verse you grave for me: Here he lies where he longed to be; Home is the sailor, home from sea, And the hunter home from the hill.&#8221;</p><footer>Requiem (Underwoods, 1887) &#183; original &#183; the lines on his grave on Mount Vaea, Samoa &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/438/pg438-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
